erland\nL35@P0: (Jean-Paul.Berrut@unifr.ch). The work of this author was supported by the Swiss National Science\nL36@P0: Foundation, grant 21–59272.99.\nL37@P0: ‡Computing Laboratory, Oxford University, Oxford, UK (LNT@comlab.ox.ac.uk).\nL38@P0: 1We are speaking here of a method of interpolating data in one space dimension by a polynomial.\nL39@P0: The “barycentric coordinates” popular in computational geometry for representing data on triangles\nL40@P0: and tetrahedra are related, but different.\nL41@P0-1: 501502 JEAN-PAUL BERRUT AND LLOYD N. TREFETHEN\nL42@P1: complex plane. Let Πn denote the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most\nL43@P1: n. The classical problem addressed here is that of finding the polynomial p ∈ Πn that\nL44@P1: interpolates f at the points xj , i.e.,\nL45@P1: p(xj )= fj ,j =0, . . . , n.\nL46@P1: The problem is well-posed; i.e., it has a unique solution that depends continuously on\nL47@P1: the data. Moreover, as explained in virtually every introductory numerical analysis\nL48@P1: text, the solution can be written in Lagrange form [44]:\nL49@P1: p(x)= \u0001n\nL50@P1: j=0\nL51@P1: fj \bj (x),\bj (x)=\nL52@P1: \u0002n\nL53@P1: k=0,k\u0004=j (x − xk)\nL54@P1: \u0002n\nL55@P1: k=0,k\u0004=j (xj − xk) (2.1) .\nL56@P1: The Lagrange polynomial \bj corresponding to the node xj has the property\nL57@P1: \bj (xk)= \u0003 1,j = k,\nL58@P1: 0, otherwise, (2.2) j, k =0, . . . , n.\nL59@P1: At this point, many authors specialize Lagrange’s formula (2.1) for small num\u0002bers n of nodes, before asserting that certain shortcomings make it a bad choice for\nL60@P1: practical computations. Among the shortcomings sometimes claimed are these:\nL61@P1: 1. Each evaluation of p(x) requires O(n2) additions and multiplications.\nL62@P1: 2. Adding a newdata pair (xn+1,fn+1) requires a newcomputation from scratch.\nL63@P1: 3. The computation is numerically unstable.\nL64@P1: From here it is commonly concluded that the Lagrange form of p is mainly a theoret\u0002ical tool for proving theorems. For computations, it is generally recommended that\nL65@P1: one should instead use Newton’s formula, which requires only O(n) flops for each\nL66@P1: evaluation of p once some numbers, which are independent of the evaluation point x,\nL67@P1: have been computed.2\nL68@P1: Newton’s approach consists of two steps. First, compute the Newton tableau of\nL69@P1: divided differences\nL70@P1: f[x0]\nL71@P1: f[x0,x1]\nL72@P1: f[x1] f[x0,x1,x2]\nL73@P1: f[x1,x2] f[x0,x1,x2,x3]\nL74@P1: f[x2] f[x1,x2,x3] ...\nL75@P1: f[x2,x3] .\nL76@P1: .\nL77@P1: . f[x0,x1,x2, . . ., xn]\nL78@P1: f[x3] .\nL79@P1: .\nL80@P1: . f[xn−3,...,xn]\nL81@P1: ...\nL82@P1: .\nL83@P1: .\nL84@P1: . f[xn−2,xn−1,xn] .\nL85@P1: .\nL86@P1: . f[xn−1,xn]\nL87@P1: f[xn]\nL88@P1: 2We define a flop—floating point operation—as a multiplication or division plus an addition or\nL89@P1: subtraction. This is quite a simplification of the reality of computer hardware, since divisions are\nL90@P1: much slower than multiplications on many processors, but this matter is not of primary importance\nL91@P1: for our discussion since we are mainly interested in the larger differences between O(n) and O(n2)\nL92@P1-2: or O(n2) and O(n3). As usual, O(np) means ≤ cnp for some constant c.BARYCENTRIC LAGRANGE INTERPOLATION 503\nL93@P2: by the recursive formula\nL94@P2: f[xj ,xj+1,...,xk−1,xk]= f[xj+1,...,xk] − f[xj ,...,xk−1]\nL95@P2: xk − xj\nL96@P2: (2.3)\nL97@P2: with initial condition f[xj ]= fj . This requires about n2 subtractions and n2/2\nL98@P2: divisions. Second, for each x, evaluate p(x) by the Newton interpolation formula\nL99@P2: p(x)= f[x0]+ f[x0,x1](x − x0)+ f[x0,x1,x2](x − x0)(x − x1)+ ···\nL100@P2: (2.4) + f[x0,x1,...,xn](x − x0)(x − x1)···(x − xn−1).\nL101@P2: This requires a mere n flops when carried out by nested multiplication, much less\nL102@P2: than the O(n2) required for direct application of (2.1).\nL103@P2: 3. An Improved Lagrange Formula. The first purpose of the present work is\nL104@P2: to emphasize that the Lagrange formula (2.1) can be rewritten in such a way that it\nL105@P2: too can be evaluated and updated in O(n) operations, just like its Newton counter\u0002part (2.4). To this end, it suffices to note that the numerator of \bj in (2.1) can be\nL106@P2: written as the quantity\nL107@P2: (3.1) \b(x)=(x − x0)(x − x1)···(x − xn)\nL108@P2: divided by x−xj .(\b is denoted by Ω or ω in many texts.) If we define the barycentric\nL109@P2: weights by3\nL110@P2: wj = 1\nL111@P2: \u0002\nL112@P2: k\u0004=j (xj − xk) (3.2) ,j =0, . . . , n,\nL113@P2: that is, wj =1/\b\u0006\nL114@P2: (xj ) [37, p. 243], we can thus write \bj as\nL115@P2: \bj (x)= \b(x) wj\nL116@P2: x − xj\nL117@P2: .\nL118@P2: Nowwe note that all the terms of the sum in (2.1) contain the factor \b(x), which does\nL119@P2: not depend on j. This factor may therefore be brought in front of the sum to yield\nL120@P2: p(x)= \b(x)\nL121@P2: \u0001n\nL122@P2: j=0\nL123@P2: wj\nL124@P2: x − xj\nL125@P2: (3.3) fj .\nL126@P2: That’s it! NowLagrange interpolation too is a formula requiring O(n2) flops for\nL127@P2: calculating some quantities independent of x, the numbers wj , followed by O(n) flops\nL128@P2: for evaluating p once these numbers are known. Rutishauser [51] called (3.3) the “first\nL129@P2: form of the barycentric interpolation formula.”\nL130@P2: What about updating? A look at (3.2) shows that incorporating a new node xn+1\nL131@P2: entails two calculations:\nL132@P2: • Divide each wj , j =0,...,n, by xj − xn+1 (one flop for each point), for a\nL133@P2: cost of n + 1 flops.\nL134@P2: • Compute wn+1 with formula (3.2), for another n + 1 flops.\nL135@P2: 3In practice, in view of the remarks of the previous footnote, one would normally choose to\nL136@P2-3: implement this formula by n multiplications followed by a single division.504 JEAN-PAUL BERRUT AND LLOYD N. TREFETHEN\nL137@P3: The Lagrange interpolant can thus also be updated with O(n) flops! By applying this\nL138@P3: update recursively while minimizing the number of divisions, we get the following\nL139@P3: algorithm for computing wj = w(n)\nL140@P3: j , j =0,...,n:\nL141@P3: w(0)\nL142@P3: 0 =1\nL143@P3: for j =1 to n do\nL144@P3: for k =0 to j − 1 do\nL145@P3: w(j)\nL146@P3: k =(xk − xj )w(j−1)k\nL147@P3: end\nL148@P3: w(j)\nL149@P3: j = \u0002j−1\nL150@P3: k=0(xj − xk)\nL151@P3: end\nL152@P3: for j =0 to n do\nL153@P3: w(j)\nL154@P3: j =1/w(j)j\nL155@P3: end\nL156@P3: This algorithm performs the same operations as (3.2), only in a different order.4\nL157@P3: A remarkable advantage of Lagrange over Newton interpolation, rarely mentioned\nL158@P3: in the literature, is that the quantities that have to be computed in O(n2) operations\nL159@P3: do not depend on the data fj . This feature permits the interpolation of as many\nL160@P3: functions as desired in O(n) operations each once the weights wj are known, whereas\nL161@P3: Newton interpolation requires the recomputation of the divided difference tableau for\nL162@P3: each newfunction.\nL163@P3: Another advantage of the Lagrange formula is that it does not depend on the order\nL164@P3: in which the nodes are arranged. In the Newton formula, the divided differences do\nL165@P3: have such a dependence. This seems odd aesthetically, and it has a computational\nL166@P3: consequence: for larger values of n, many orderings lead to numerical instability. For\nL167@P3: stability, it is necessary to select the points in a van der Corput or Leja sequence or in\nL168@P3: another related sequence based on a certain equidistribution property [18, 23, 61, 67].\nL169@P3: This is not to say that Newton interpolation has no advantages. One is that it\nL170@P3: leads to elegant methods for incorporating information on derivatives f(p)\nL171@P3: (xj ) when\nL172@P3: solving so-called Hermite interpolation problems. Another may be its ability to ac\u0002commodate vector or matrix interpolation from scalar data [60]; using a representation\nL173@P3: like (3.3) would require costly matrix inversion. There are also some theoretical in\u0002stances in which Newton interpolation is preferable, e.g., the construction of multistep\nL174@P3: formulas for the solution of ordinary differential equations [33, 45].\nL175@P3: 4. The Barycentric Formula. Equation (3.3) is not the end of the story: it can\nL176@P3: be modified to an even more elegant formula, the one that is often used in practice.\nL177@P3: Suppose we interpolate, besides the data fj , the constant function 1, whose in\u0002terpolant is of course itself. Inserting into (3.3), we get\nL178@P3: 1= \u0001n\nL179@P3: j=0\nL180@P3: \bj (x)= \b(x)\nL181@P3: \u0001n\nL182@P3: j=0\nL183@P3: wj\nL184@P3: x − xj\nL185@P3: (4.1) .\nL186@P3: Dividing (3.3) by this expression and cancelling the common factor \b(x), we obtain\nL187@P3: 4This contrasts with a somewhat faster but unstable [28, p. 96] algorithm that takes advantage\nL188@P3: of the relation \u0004j\nL189@P3: k=0 w(j)\nL190@P3-4: k = 0 [67].BARYCENTRIC LAGRANGE INTERPOLATION 505\nL191@P4: the barycentric formula for p:\nL192@P4: p(x)=\nL193@P4: \u0001n\nL194@P4: j=0\nL195@P4: wj\nL196@P4: x − xj\nL197@P4: fj\nL198@P4: \u0001n\nL199@P4: j=0\nL200@P4: wj\nL201@P4: x − xj\nL202@P4: BARYCENTRIC\nL203@P4: FORMULA (4.2)\nL204@P4: where wj is still defined by (3.2). Rutishauser [51] called (4.2) the “second (true) form\nL205@P4: of the barycentric formula.” See p. 229 of [39] for comments on this terminology.\nL206@P4: We see that the barycentric formula is a Lagrange formula, but one with a special\nL207@P4: and beautiful symmetry. The weights wj appear in the denominator exactly as in the\nL208@P4: numerator, except without the data factors fj . This means that any common factor\nL209@P4: in all the weights wj may be cancelled without affecting the value of pn, and in the\nL210@P4: discussion to followwe will use this freedom.\nL211@P4: Like (3.3), (4.2) can also take advantage of the updating of the weights wj in\nL212@P4: O(n) flops to incorporate a newdata pair (xn+1,fn+1).\nL213@P4: 5. Chebyshev and Other Point Distributions. For certain special sets of nodes\nL214@P4: xj , one can give explicit formulas for the barycentric weights wj . The obvious place\nL215@P4: to start is equidistant nodes with spacing h =2/n on the interval [−1, 1]. Here the\nL216@P4: weights can be directly calculated to be wj =(−1)n−j\nL217@P4: \u0005n\nL218@P4: j\nL219@P4: \u0006\u0007(hnn!) [55], which after\nL220@P4: cancelling the factors independent of j yields\nL221@P4: wj =(−1)j\nL222@P4: \bn\nL223@P4: j\nL224@P4: \nL225@P4: (5.1) .\nL226@P4: For the interval [a, b] we would multiply the original formula for wj by 2n(b − a)−n,\nL227@P4: but this constant factor too can be dropped, so we end up with (5.1) again, regardless\nL228@P4: of a and b.\nL229@P4: A glance at (5.1) reveals that if n is large, the weights wj for equispaced barycen\u0002tric interpolation vary by exponentially large factors, of order approximately 2n. This\nL230@P4: sounds dangerous, and it is. The effect will be that even small data near the center of\nL231@P4: the interval are associated with large oscillations in the interpolant, on the order of 2n\nL232@P4: times bigger, near the edge of the interval [40, 65]. This so-called Runge phenomenon\nL233@P4: is not a problem with the barycentric formula, but is intrinsic in the underlying in\u0002terpolation problem. Among other things it implies that polynomial interpolation in\nL234@P4: equally spaced points is highly ill-conditioned: small changes in the data may cause\nL235@P4: huge changes in the interpolant.\nL236@P4: For polynomial interpolation to be a well-conditioned process, unless n is rather\nL237@P4: small, one must dispense with equally spaced points [59, Thm. 6.21.3]. As is well\nL238@P4: known in approximation theory, the right approach is to use point sets that are clus\u0002tered at the endpoints of the interval with an asymptotic density proportional to\nL239@P4: (1 − x2)−1/2 as n →∞. Remarkably, this is the same asymptotic density one gets if\nL240@P4: [−1, 1] is interpreted as a conducting wire and the points xj are interpreted as point\nL241@P4: charges that repel one another with an inverse-linear force and that are allowed to\nL242@P4: move along the wire to find their equilibrium configuration [64, Chap. 5]. And it\nL272@P5: For all of these sets of Chebyshev points, if the interval [−1, 1] is linearly trans\u0002formed to [a, b], the weights as defined by (3.2) all get multiplied by 2n(b − a)−n.\nL273@P5: However, as this factor cancels out in the barycentric formula, there is again no need\nL274@P5: to include it (and it is safer not to—see section 7).\nL275@P5: One sees that, with equidistant or Chebyshev points, no expensive computations\nL276@P5: are needed to get the weights wj , and thus only O(n) operations are required for\nL277@P5: evaluating pn. No other interpolation method seems to achieve this, for as mentioned\nL278@P5: in section 3, Newton interpolation always requires O(n2) operations for the divided\nL279@P5: differences.\nL280@P5: Here is a Matlab code segment that samples the function f(x)= |x| + x/2 − x2\nL281@P5: in 1001 Chebyshev points of the second kind and evaluates the barycentric interpolant\nL282@P5: in 5000 points. One would rarely use so many points in practice; we pick such large\nL283@P5: values just to illustrate the effectiveness of the formula. (At the end of section 7 we\nL284@P5: shall modify this program to avoid failure when x = xi.)\nL285@P5: n = 1000;\nL286@P5: fun = inline(’abs(x)+.5*x-x.ˆ2’);\nL287@P5: x = cos(pi*(0:n)’/n);\nL288@P5: f = fun(x);\nL289@P5-6: c = [1/2; ones(n-1,1); 1/2].*(-1).ˆ((0:n)’);BARYCENTRIC LAGRANGE INTERPOLATION 507\nL290@P6: −1 −0.5 0 0.5 1\nL291@P6: −0.5\nL292@P6: 0\nL293@P6: 0.5\nL294@P6: n=20\nL295@P6: −1 −0.5 0 0.5 1\nL296@P6: −0.5\nL297@P6: 0\nL298@P6: 0.5\nL299@P6: n=100\nL300@P6: Fig. 5.1 Barycentric interpolation of the function f(x)= |x| + x/2 − x2 in 21 and 101 Chebyshev\nL377@P8: changes in the data may cause large changes in the interpolant, typically manifested\nL378@P8: as large oscillations near the endpoints. Unless the nodes converge to the (1−x2)−1/2\nL379@P8: distribution, the condition numbers growexponentially as n →∞. In such cir\u0002cumstances polynomial interpolation is usually not a good method for applications,\nL380@P8: regardless of whether one uses a Lagrange, Newton, or any other formulation. Even\nL381@P8: though the interpolants may converge to f in theory as n →∞ when f is sufficiently\nL804@P15: and differentiation, SIAM J. Numer. Anal., 33 (1996), pp. 1689–1711.\nL805@P15: [22] K. Eriksson, D. Estep, P. Hansbo, and C. Johnson, Introduction to Computational Methods\nL806@P15: for Differential Equations, Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1995.\nL807@P15: [23] B. Fischer and L. Reichel, Newton interpolation in Fej´er and Chebyshev points, Math.\nL808@P15: Comp., 53 (1989), pp. 265–278.\nL809@P15: [24] B. Fornberg, A Practical Guide to Pseudospectral Methods, Cambridge University Press,\nL810@P15: Cambridge, UK, 1996.\nL857@P16: Stuttgart, 1976; English translation, Lectures on Numerical Mathematics, Walter Gautschi,\nL858@P16: ed., Birkh¨auser, Boston, 1990.\nL859@P16: [52] H. E. Salzer, Lagrangian interpolation at the Chebyshev points xn,ν, = cos(νπ/n), ν = 0(1)n;\nL860@P16: some unnoted advantages, Comput. J., 15 (1972), pp. 156–159.\nL861@P16: [53] R. Sauer and I. Szabo´, Mathematische Hilfsmittel des Ingenieurs, Springer-Verlag, Berlin,\nL862@P16: Heidelberg, 1968.\nL863@P16: [54] C. Schneider and W. Werner, Some new aspects of rational interpolation, Math. Comp., 47\nL864@P16: (1986), pp. 285–299.\nL865@P16: [55] H. R. Schwarz, Numerische Mathematik, 4th ed., Teubner, Stuttgart, 1997; English transla\u0002tion of the 2nd edition, Numerical Analysis: A Comprehensive Introduction, Wiley, New\nL866@P16: York, 1989.\nL867@P16: [56] F. Stenger, Numerical Metho