ynomial must be manipulated through the formulas of\nL19@P0: barycentric interpolation. Barycentric interpolation is not new, but most students,\nL20@P0: most mathematical scientists, and even many numerical analysts do not knowabout\nL21@P0: it. This simple and powerful idea deserves a place at the heart of introductory courses\nL22@P0: and textbooks in numerical analysis.1\nL23@P0: As always with polynomial interpolation, unless the degree of the polynomial is\nL24@P0: low, it is usually not a good idea to use uniformly spaced interpolation points. Instead\nL25@P0: one should use Chebyshev points or other systems of points clustered at the boundary\nL26@P0: of the interval of approximation.\nL27@P0: 2. Lagrange and Newton Interpolation. Let n + 1 distinct interpolation points\nL28@P0: (nodes) xj , j =0,...,n, be given, together with corresponding numbers fj , which\nL29@P0: may or may not be samples of a function f. Unless stated otherwise, we assume\nL30@P0: that the nodes are real, although most of our results and comments generalize to the\nL31@P0: ∗Received by the editors November 8, 2002; accepted for publication (in revised form) December\nL32@P0: 24, 2003; published electronically July 30, 2004.\nL33@P0: http://www.siam.org/journals/sirev/46-3/41771.html\nL34@P0: †D´epartement de Math´ematiques, Universit´e de Fribourg, 1700 Fribourg/P´erolles, Switzerland\nL35@P0: (Jean-Paul.Berrut@unifr.ch). The work of this author was supported by the Swiss National Science\nL36@P0: Foundation, grant 21–59272.99.\nL37@P0: ‡Computing Laboratory, Oxford University, Oxford, UK (LNT@comlab.ox.ac.uk).\nL38@P0: 1We are speaking here of a method of interpolating data in one space dimension by a polynomial.\nL39@P0: The “barycentric coordinates” popular in computational geometry for representing data on triangles\nL40@P0: and tetrahedra are related, but different.\nL41@P0-1: 501502 JEAN-PAUL BERRUT AND LLOYD N. TREFETHEN\nL42@P1: complex plane. Let Πn denote the vector space of 